{
  "notes": "Phase 167.1.2.1.1 WRITERRESIDUALS, SC-1 (decision D-04). ONE table for three consumers, so the Python writer, the TS reader and the SQL census mirror cannot drift apart silently. When the allocator equity derive REFUSES to publish a curve it no longer deletes the (allocator_id, 'equity_curve') row: it upserts a version-2 refusal row (is_trustworthy false, curve [], returns [], degrade_reasons carrying the token, refusal set to the token) that says why. `refusals` has one row per live DeriveRefusal member (7 since Phase 183 D-01 retired the marked-duplicate refusal): `payload` is exactly what refusal_payload(token, composed_at=composed_at, extra_reasons=extra_reasons) returns, and `reader_reason` is the EquityHistoryRebuildReason untrustworthyRebuildReason (src/lib/queries.ts) maps that row to. The reader maps each token onto a line the owner already sees; no new copy exists for any of them. analytics-service/tests/test_derive_refusal_rows.py asserts the payloads, src/lib/__tests__/derive-refusal-rows.test.ts asserts the reader, and src/__tests__/accounttruth-census.test.ts evaluates the census CASE arms (scripts/accounttruth-census.mjs, untrustworthy_reason) over the same rows. `blocking` has one row per DegradeReason in _BLOCKING_REASONS (an untrustworthy composed curve carrying that single token) and the line the reader gives it: the two shared-account tokens keep their own line, failing_key_series_ended (167.1.2.1.1 SC-2) reads shared_account_no_working_key, shared_account_flows_disagree (SC-3) reads shared_account_history_truncated, account_identity_pending (SC-6) reads account_identity_pending, every other value reads derivation_rejected. `precedence` holds multi-token degrade_reasons arrays whose order the reader must not depend on: the most actionable cause wins (shared_account_no_working_key or failing_key_series_ended, then an identity collision, then account_identity_pending, then shared_account_history_truncated or shared_account_flows_disagree), and compose_incomplete or compose_empty reads as a wait (awaiting_derivation) ONLY when it is the sole token, so a wait token next to dropped_key is a refusal. A brand-new book (every key anchored, none with a return day yet) persists the bare compose_empty row, not compose_empty_blocked (MR-01), so it reads as the wait it is. `legacy_refusals` lists tokens a stored pre-Phase-183 row may still carry (the derive no longer produces them, because the account-group resolution counts a marked duplicate once), each with the line the reader gives it; no legacy token is a live DeriveRefusal member, and every consumer must keep mapping it until the next derive replaces the stored row (Phase 183 D-01). The precedence rows that used to name the retired token name account_identity_collision, which reads the same line.",
